      "side": 1,
      "message": "uv is typically for nv12/nv21.  the uv plane follows the y plane.  the y plane can be 1 byte or odd, followed by uv plane which is unaligned.\n\nbut all cpus that i know allow unaligned 16 bit values.  I think this is ubsan and an explite warning added for unaligned int/short references?\nis the a real hardware issue?\n\nalthough it is uint8 for u and v, nv12 can be considered big endian.  u followed by v.  and nv21 is little endian v followed by u.\nandroid considered these to be \u0027planes\u0027 of u and v with a \u0027pixel stride\u0027 of 1 or -1 but that does not implement well.\nwe could go back to reading bytes for this use case, but for 16 bit rgb565 and 2 bit ar30 it makes more sense to treat them as integers with bit fields.\n\nfor simd there are different issues with neon and sse.  but the assembly does unaligned loads/stores.\n\ni\u0027m reluctant to add 2 byte memcpy because in the past, or with build options, it really does push 2 pointers and count and make a function call, which is alot more code and slower.  This change relies on a modern compiler and build options to avoid that.",
      "revId": "b64b89ab8b30888ecf94146d99ca89b30be3d042",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
